{:edb/db-before-t 155 :edb/db-after-t 156 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533468 50 #inst "2026-10-10T23:49:16.815Z" 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186157758 1068 #edb/ref 17592186155356 13194139533468 17592186045585 false] [17592186157758 1068 #edb/ref 17592186155362 13194139533468 17592186045585 false] [17592186157758 1068 #edb/ref 17592186155365 13194139533468 17592186045585 false] [17592186157758 1068 #edb/ref 17592186155371 13194139533468 17592186045585 false] [17592186157758 1068 #edb/ref 17592186155377 13194139533468 17592186045585 false] [17592186157758 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396368 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186157758 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396372 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186157758 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396378 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186157758 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396384 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186157758 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396387 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186157758 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186157758 1071 "Consider the following statement.\n\n> If $f(x) = 3e^{2x} + 2x$, then $f = O(e^{2x})$ as $x → ∞$.\n\nA proof of the statement is given below\n\n> $L1:f = O(3e^{2x} + 2x)$\n> $L2:lim_(x → ∞)(\\frac{2x}{3e^{2x}}) = 0$\n> $L3:2x = O(3e^{2x})$\n> $L4:f = O(3e^{2x})$\n> $L5:f = O(e^{2x})$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\nLine $L1$ follows from {{field-1}}.\n\nLine $L4$ follows from lines\n{{field-2}} and\n{{field-3}}.\n\nLine $L5$ follows from line {{field-4}} and\n{{field-5}}." 13194139533468 17592186045585 false] [17592186157758 1071 "Consider the following statement. \n\nIf $f(x)=3{e}^{2x}+2x,$ then $f=O({e}^{2x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nA proof of the statement is given below\n\n $\\text{L1}:\\,f=O(3{e}^{2x}+2x)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2x}{3{e}^{2x}})=0$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,2x=O(3{e}^{2x})$ \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,f=O(3{e}^{2x})$  \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,f=O({e}^{2x})$ \n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ follows from {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from lines \n{{field-2}} and \n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from line {{field-4}}  and \n{{field-5}}." 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186157758 1074 "Suppose $f(x),g(x),h(x)$ and $k(x)$ are functions and $c$ is a nonzero constant. Then, we have the following properties of Big-O notation. \n\n- The sum and product properties. If\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(k)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen\n\n$$\nf+g=O(h+k)\\,\\text{and}\\,fg=O(hk)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The constant multiple property. If \n\n$$\nf=O(cg)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The reflexive property. \n\n$$\nf=O(f)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n- The transitive property. If \n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The absorption property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(g+h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen \n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L1},$ which states that\n\n$$\nf=O({{3{e}^{2x}+2x}_{⏟}}_{f})\n$$\n\nand this is true by $\\text{the reflexive property}.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L4}.$ From line $\\text{L1},$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(3{e}^{2x}+2x)\n$$\n\nand from line $\\text{L3},$ we have\n\n$$\n2x=O(3{e}^{2x}).\n$$\n\nThus, by lines $\\text{L1 and L3}$ and the $\\text{absorption property},$ we have \n\n$$\nf=O(3{e}^{2x}+2x)=O(3{e}^{2x}).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\text{L5}.$ From line $\\text{L4},$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(3{e}^{2x})\n$$\n\nand by the $\\text{constant multiple property}$ with $c=3,$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O({e}^{2x}).\n$$" 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186166928 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396542 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186167254 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164601 13194139533468 17592186045585 false] [17592186167254 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164604 13194139533468 17592186045585 false] [17592186167254 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164607 13194139533468 17592186045585 false] [17592186167254 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164611 13194139533468 17592186045585 false] [17592186167254 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396468 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186167254 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396471 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186167254 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396474 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186167254 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396477 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186167254 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186167254 1071 "![](images/71/718df02f2d0c2f01cc4a02a40da120178afe0f436bbf38cd5a8d249d87b5d1c9.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait above showing a solution of a Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=2x-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-6y+2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x=x(t),y=y(t),$ and $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are positive constants.\n\nSelect the options below that best describe the dynamics of the system from point $A$ to point $B$ on the solution.\n\n$\\,$\nThe trajectory \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$\nThe number of prey {{field-3}} and the number of predators {{field-4}}." 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186167254 1071 "![](images/71/718df02f2d0c2f01cc4a02a40da120178afe0f436bbf38cd5a8d249d87b5d1c9.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait above showing a solution of a Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model\n${x^{′} = 2x - xy; y^{′} =-6y + 2xy$\nwhere $x = x(t), y = y(t)$, and $a, b, c$, and $d$ are positive constants.\n\nSelect the options below that best describe the dynamics of the system from point $A$ to point $B$ on the solution.\n\nThe trajectory\n{{field-1}} {{field-2}}.\n\nThe number of prey {{field-3}} and the number of predators {{field-4}}." 13194139533468 17592186045585 false] [17592186167254 1074 "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model is given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d$ are positive real constants. Here, $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements:\n\n- The trajectory $\\text{circles around}$ $\\text{the nonzero equilibrium}.$\n\n- From $A$ to $B,$ the number of prey $\\text{decreases}$ and the number of predators $\\text{increases}.$" 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186167263 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164703 13194139533468 17592186045585 false] [17592186167263 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164706 13194139533468 17592186045585 false] [17592186167263 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164709 13194139533468 17592186045585 false] [17592186167263 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164713 13194139533468 17592186045585 false] [17592186167263 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396549 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186167263 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396552 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186167263 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396555 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186167263 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396559 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186167263 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186167263 1071 "![](images/35/359b56411d4e215ef0ed423f6280a9e3641d5806d525b78e97569aaa02afe72f.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait above showing a solution of a Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=8x-4xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-6y+2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x=x(t),y=y(t),$ and $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are positive constants.\n\nSelect the options below that best describe the dynamics of the system from point $A$ to point $B$ on the solution.\n\n$\\,$\nThe trajectory \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$\nThe number of prey {{field-3}} and the number of predators {{field-4}}." 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186167263 1071 "![](images/35/359b56411d4e215ef0ed423f6280a9e3641d5806d525b78e97569aaa02afe72f.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait above showing a solution of a Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model\n${x^{′} = 8x - 4xy; y^{′} =-6y + 2xy$\nwhere $x = x(t), y = y(t)$, and $a, b, c$, and $d$ are positive constants.\n\nSelect the options below that best describe the dynamics of the system from point $A$ to point $B$ on the solution.\n\nThe trajectory\n{{field-1}} {{field-2}}.\n\nThe number of prey {{field-3}} and the number of predators {{field-4}}." 13194139533468 17592186045585 false] [17592186167263 1074 "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model is given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d$ are positive real constants. Here, $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements:\n\n- The trajectory $\\text{circles around}$ $\\text{the nonzero equilibrium}.$\n\n- From $A$ to $B,$ the number of prey $\\text{decreases}$ and the number of predators $\\text{decreases}.$" 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186198469 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396288 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186198469 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396301 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186198470 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396314 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186198471 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396260 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186198471 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396278 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186198850 1068 #edb/ref 17592186196111 13194139533468 17592186045585 false] [17592186198850 1068 #edb/ref 17592186196117 13194139533468 17592186045585 false] [17592186198850 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396250 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186198850 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396256 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186198850 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186198850 1071 "Describe the set $S = {…, 3, 6, 12, 24, 48,...}$ using a constructive definition.\n\n*Hint: Take $a_{1} = 6$ in the sequence that generates the elements of $S$.*\n\n$2^{n}$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}" 13194139533468 17592186045585 false] [17592186198850 1071 "Describe the set $S={…,3,6,12,24,48,...}$ using a constructive definition.\n\n*Hint: Take ${a}_{1}=6$ in the sequence that generates the elements of $S.$*\n\n$$\nS={ {{field-1}}\\,:\\,n\\in {{field-2}} }\n$$" 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186198850 1074 "Notice that the elements of our set\n\n$$\n…,\\,3,\\,6,\\,12,\\,24,\\,48,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a two-way infinite geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =2 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{12} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting  ${a}_{1}=6,$ the $n$th term ${a}_{n}$ of our sequence for $n\\in Z$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =6\\cdot {2}^{n-1} \\\\  & =6\\cdot {2}^{n}\\cdot {2}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{2}\\cdot {2}^{n} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can define our set using a constructive definition as follows:\n\n$$\nS={3\\cdot {2}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in Z}\n$$" 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186221408 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396343 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186221408 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396393 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186221408 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396418 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186221408 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396443 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186221423 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396533 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186221423 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396562 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186221435 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396481 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186221435 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396508 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186221442 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396324 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186222010 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220697 13194139533468 17592186045585 false] [17592186222010 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220701 13194139533468 17592186045585 false] [17592186222010 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220706 13194139533468 17592186045585 false] [17592186222010 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396495 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186222010 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396499 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186222010 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396503 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186222010 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186222010 1071 "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0, ∞)$ and its Laplace transform\n$F(s) = L{f(t)}$\nexists for $s > a$. Then, $F(s)$ is twice differentiable at every point $s > a$, and we have\n$F^{″}(s) = L{t^{2} \\cdot f(t)}$.\n\nA proof of this statement is given below.\n\n> $L1$: $F(s) = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{∞}e^{-st}f(t)dt$ exists for $s > a$.\n> $L2$: $F^{″}(s) = \\frac{d^{2}}{ds^{2}}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{∞}e^{-st}f(t)dt$\n> $L3$: $F^{″}(s) = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{∞}\\frac{∂^{2}}{∂s^{2}}(e^{-st}f(t))dt$\n> $L4$: $\\frac{∂^{2}}{∂s^{2}}(e^{-st}f(t)) = t^{2}e^{-st}f(t)$\n> $L5$: $F^{″}(s) = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{∞}t^{2}e^{-st}f(t)dt$\n> $L6$: $F^{″}(s) = L{t^{2} \\cdot f(t)}$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nLine $L2$ follows from line $L1$ and\n{{field-1}}.\n\nLine $L3$ follows from $L2$ and {{field-2}}.\n\nLine $L4$ follows from {{field-3}}." 13194139533468 17592186045585 false] [17592186222010 1071 "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for $s>a.$ Then, $F(s)$ is twice differentiable at every point $s>a,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}.\n$$\n\nA proof of this statement is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ $F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$ exists for $s>a.$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))={t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ and\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ and {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from {{field-3}}." 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186222010 1074 "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. \n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and $s>a,$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}.\n$$\n\nWe're asked to prove the smoothness property in the case $n=2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L2}.$ From line $\\text{L1}$ we know that\n\n$$\nF(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nBy $\\text{the definition of the second derivative with respect to }s$, we get\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}F(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhich is exactly the statement in line $\\text{L2}.$\n\n- Next we consider line $\\text{L3}.$ From $\\text{L2}$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is of exponential order, the integral converges, and we can\ndifferentiate under the integral sign:\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt.\n$$\n\nTherefore, line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{differentiating under the integral sign}.$\n\n- We now consider line $\\text{L4}.$ The function $f(t)$ does not depend on $s$, so\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))=f(t)\\,\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st})=f(t)\\,\\frac{∂}{∂s}(-t{e}^{-st})=f(t)\\,({t}^{2}{e}^{-st})={t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t).\n$$\n\nThus line $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{the chain rule}.$" 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186229137 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396522 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186229138 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396571 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186396250 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396251 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186396250 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396252 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186396250 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396253 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186396250 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396254 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186396250 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396255 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186396250 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186396250 1077 "field-1" 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186396250 1079 #edb/ref 17592186396252 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186396250 1080 #uuid "3da75658-493e-5414-9ef4-07db0edd9ac6" 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186396251 1081 "\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {2}^{n}" 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186396251 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186396251 1084 #uuid "6756d5e9-8c2b-5648-9cef-d57cf02e8e50" 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186396252 1081 "3\\cdot {2}^{n}" 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186396252 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186396252 1084 #uuid "de50addf-3149-5f58-b066-97f2f40179d2" 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186396253 1081 "\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {3}^{n}" 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186396253 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186396253 1084 #uuid "cf715dcc-e03e-5ea8-aeed-f7ac4fdc33cc" 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [