 17592186045585 true] [17592186396418 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186396418 1071 "The local truncation error (LTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{5}-(Δx)^{6}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.4,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.6$ to $Δx=0.4,$ the LTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186396418 1072 #uuid "191ac454-645f-542b-be97-450b5f3d1a65" 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186396418 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the LTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.6) & =6(0.6)^{5}-(0.6)^{6} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 0.077\\,76-0.046\\,656 \\\\  & =0.466\\,56-0.046\\,656 \\\\  & =0.419\\,904 \\\\  & ≈0.420.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.4,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.4) & =6(0.4)^{5}-(0.4)^{6} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 0.010\\,24-0.004\\,096 \\\\  & =0.061\\,44-0.004\\,096 \\\\  & =0.057\\,344 \\\\  & ≈0.057.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.6$ to $0.4.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.6}{0.4}=1.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={1.5}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.419\\,904$ to $0.057\\,344.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.419\\,904}{0.057\\,344}=7.322\\,54…≈7.323.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1.5}^{p+1} & ≈7.322\\,54… \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{1.5}⁡(7.322\\,54…) \\\\ p+1 & ≈4.910… \\\\ p & ≈3.910….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$4.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the LTE:\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{5}-(Δx)^{6}\\,⇒\\,τ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{5})=O\\,((Δx)^{4+1}),\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $4.$" 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186396419 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396420 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186396419 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396421 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186396419 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396422 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186396419 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396423 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186396419 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396424 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186396419 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186396419 1077 "field-1" 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186396419 1079 #edb/ref 17592186396421 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186396419 1080 #uuid "ba367254-5de2-5eff-9aa7-acecd1860277" 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186396420 1081 "0.057" 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186396420 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186396420 1084 #uuid "17cc96c1-7c53-5909-a9eb-4804b7e498d3" 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186396421 1081 "0.420" 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186396421 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592186396421 1084 #uuid "2eb02a74-9fae-5992-999d-2d580bdb32f8" 13194139533468 17592186045585 true] [17592